C o m p i l a t i o n


      USU
      CEE 6410
      Homework 2
      Irrigation Problem
       
      An aqueduct constructed to supply water to industrial users has an excess capacity in the months
      of June, July, and August of 14,000 acft, 18,000 acft, and 6,000 acft, respectively. It is
      proposed to develop not more than 10,000 acres of new land by utilizing the excess aqueduct
      capacity for irrigation water deliveries. Two crops, hay and grain, are to be grown. Their
      monthly water requirements and expected net returns are given in the following table:
      Monthly Water Requirement (acft/acre)
              June July August    Return, $/acre
      Hay       2    1    1            100
      Grain     1    2    0            120
       
       
      Ammon Wallace
      Ammon Wallace
      CEE 6410
      ammon.wallace@usu.edu
  23   
  24   
  25  * 1. Define Sets
  26  Sets
  27  crop different crops /Hay, Grain/
  28  t months /June, July, August/;
  29   
  30  * 2. Define Parameters
  31  Parameters
  32  obj_fun_coef(crop) objective function coef. ($ per acre) /Hay 100, Grain 120/
  33  irr(t) the volume of water (acre-ft) accessible each month /June 14000, July 18000, August 6000/
  34  max_development the maximimum development (acres) /10000/;
  35   
  36  * 3. Define C matrix
  37  Table C(crop, t) Water requirements for each crop per month
  38          June    July    August
  39  Hay       2       1        1
  40  Grain     1       2        0;
  41   
  42  * 4. Define Variables
  43  Positive Variables
  44  Y_land Dual objective function variable [$ per acre]
  45  Y(t) Dual objective function variables [S per acre-ft]
  46  X(crop) Primal objective function variables [amount of hay and grain in acres];
  47   
  48  Variables
  49  MaxProfit maximize primal obj. function
  50  MinProfit minimize dual obj. function;
  51   
  52  * 5. Define Equations
  53  Equations
  54  Primal_obj Primal objective function
  55  Dual_obj Dual objective function
  56  Primal_land Primal Land constraint
  57  Primal_irr Primal irrigation constraint
  58  Dual_con Dual constraints equation;
  59   
  60   
  61  * Objective Functions
  62  Primal_obj.. sum(crop, obj_fun_coef(crop)*X(crop)) =E= MaxProfit;
  63  Dual_obj.. Y_land*max_development + sum(t, Y(t)*irr(t)) =E= MinProfit;
  64   
  65  * Primal Constraints
  66  Primal_land.. sum(crop, X(crop)) =L= max_development;
  67  Primal_irr(t).. sum(crop, C(crop,t)*X(crop)) =L= irr(t);
  68   
  69  * Dual Constraints
  70  Dual_con(crop).. Y_land+sum(t, C(crop,t)*Y(t)) =G= obj_fun_coef(crop);
  71   
  72   
  73  * 6. Create Models
  74  Model Primal /Primal_obj, Primal_land, Primal_irr/;
  75  Model Dual /Dual_obj, Dual_con/;
  76   
  77  * 7. Solve Models
  78  Solve Primal using LP maximizing MaxProfit;
  79  Solve Dual using LP minimizing MinProfit;
  80   


